// Requested DLSS Frame Generation multiplier, in multiplier units (2 = 2x,
// i.e. one interpolated frame per rendered frame, ... 6 = 6x).  Stored the way
// the settings UI presents it and converted to
// DLSSGOptions::numFramesToGenerate (multiplier - 1) at apply time.
//
// A request above what the driver reports is NOT rejected: an Ada part without
// the multi-frame unlock answers numFramesToGenerateMax = 1, and degrading such
// a request to plain 2x keeps frame generation working.  getFGMultiplierMax()
// reports the ceiling so the settings screen can show the clamp.
//
// 0 = off (2026-10-11).  A request of 0 does not mean "generate nothing per
// frame"; it means the feature is not wanted right now, so the present path
// takes the same DLSSGMode::eOff route it already takes for a full-screen menu
// (see fgDesiredOn).  Without this the lowest selectable step was 2x, i.e. one
// generated frame and one extra present per rendered frame, which is the wrong
// baseline for measuring anything that is paid per presented frame.
static std::atomic<int> g_fgMultiplierRequested{2};
// Highest numFramesToGenerate the DLSS-G plugin reported (1 == 2x).  Written by
// the enable path and by the periodic [FG-1 STATE] query, and used to re-apply
// the options once the ceiling changes.
static std::atomic<uint32_t> g_fgGeneratedMaxSeen{1};
// Base frame-rate cap for frame generation, in rendered frames per second:
//   -1 = automatic pairing (display refresh / multiplier)
//    0 = no cap
//   >0 = that many rendered frames per second
//
// Frame generation multiplies whatever the renderer hands it, so an uncapped
// renderer feeds the display far more frames than it can show: on a 165 Hz
// panel at 6x, anything past ~27 fps of rendering is generated and then thrown
// away, and the latency is paid for the whole time.  The cap goes through the
// Reflex frame limiter because that is the pacing path Streamline documents for
// use with DLSS-G.
static int g_fgBaseFrameLimitRequest = -1;
// Hz actually handed to Reflex (0 = unlimited).  Mirrors the request after
// resolution, so the settings screen can show what "automatic" resolved to.
static std::atomic<uint32_t> g_fgBaseFrameLimitHz{0};
// True while a full-screen menu or the main menu is up.  Pushed from the client
// every frame.  Frame generation is switched off for those frames, and the base
// frame-rate cap is lifted with it, because the pairing only means anything
// while generated frames exist.
static std::atomic<bool> g_fgFullscreenMenuOpen{false};
